  "generated_text": "1. Determine the possible sums when rolling two 6-sided dice:\n   - The minimum sum is 2 (1+1).\n   - The maximum sum is 12 (6+6).\n\n2. Identify the perfect squares within this range:\n   - Perfect squares between 2 and 12 are: 4, 9, 16.\n\n3. Count the number of ways to achieve each perfect square:\n   - Sum = 4: (1,3), (3,1)\n   - Sum = 9: (4,5), (5,4)\n   - Sum = 16: (6,6)\n\n4. Calculate the total number of favorable outcomes:\n   - Total favorable outcomes = 2 (for sum 4) + 2 (for sum 9) + 1 (for sum 16) = 5\n\n5. Calculate the probability:\n   - Probability = Number of favorable outcomes / Total possible outcomes\n   - Probability = 5 / 36\n\nTherefore, the probability that the sum rolled is a perfect square is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{36}}\\)",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[5/36, '\\\\frac{5}{36}']",
    "gold_answer": "Rolling two dice has $6 \\times 6=36$ possible outcomes.  The only perfect squares that we can roll are 4 and 9. Pairs adding up to 4 are 1+3, 2+2, and 3+1. Those that add up to 9 are 3+6, 4+5, 5+4, and 6+3. The answer is $\\boxed{\\dfrac{7}{36}}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
